    "clinical_notes": [
      {
        "type": "Surgical Note",
        "author": "Dr. Sarah Mitchell, Orthopedic Surgery",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-09T14:30:00",
        "title": "Operative Report \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Right Total Knee Arthroplasty",
        "content": "PROCEDURE: Right total knee arthroplasty.\nINDICATION: Severe right knee osteoarthritis, failed conservative management.\nANESTHESIA: Spinal with sedation.\nEBL: 350 mL. Tourniquet time 82 minutes.\nIMPLANT: Stryker Triathlon CR size 5.\nFINDINGS: Severe tricompartmental arthritis with eburnated bone. Good ligament balance achieved.\nCOMPLICATIONS: None.\nPLAN: Weight-bearing as tolerated, CPM, DVT prophylaxis per protocol, PT starting POD#1.\nNOTE: Patient has allergy to heparin listed \u00e2\u20ac\u201d per pharmacy, allergy documented as HIT in 2023. Discussed with patient who states he was told to avoid heparin. Orthopedic team ordered enoxaparin initially, later transitioned to unfractionated heparin by covering team on POD#1 for aPTT monitoring. [NOTE: The allergy alert was overridden in the system.]"
      },
      {
        "type": "Progress Note",
        "author": "Dr. Kevin Zhao, PGY-2, Internal Medicine (covering)",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-10T08:00:00",
        "title": "POD#1 Progress Note",
        "content": "67M POD#1 s/p R TKA. Hemodynamically stable. Pain controlled with PCA. Started PT, walked 10 feet with walker. Transitioned DVT prophylaxis from enoxaparin to unfractionated heparin drip for aPTT monitoring \u00e2\u20ac\u201d initial aPTT subtherapeutic at 52, rate increased per nomogram. Diet advanced to regular. Foley removed. I&O adequate.\nA/P: Progressing well. Continue current plan. Target aPTT 60-80."
      },
      {
        "type": "Progress Note",
        "author": "Dr. Sarah Mitchell, Orthopedic Surgery",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-12T09:00:00",
        "title": "POD#3 Progress Note",
        "content": "Patient progressing well. Walking 200 feet with walker, knee ROM 0-85 degrees. Incision clean/dry/intact. Hemoglobin stable at 11.4. Heparin drip therapeutic, aPTT 64. Pain improving, decreasing PCA use. Anticipate discharge POD#6-7 if continues to progress.\nNote: Platelet count 178, down from 220 on POD#1. Will monitor."
      },
      {
        "type": "Progress Note",
        "author": "Dr. Sarah Mitchell, Orthopedic Surgery",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-14T09:00:00",
        "title": "POD#5 Progress Note",
        "content": "Continued progress with PT \u00e2\u20ac\u201d walking 400 feet, stairs with rail. ROM 0-95 degrees. Hemoglobin trended down to 10.1, no obvious source \u00e2\u20ac\u201d expected post-surgical trend. Platelets 132, continuing to trend down \u00e2\u20ac\u201d attributed to hemodilution and heparin effect. BUN slightly elevated at 24. Plan for discharge tomorrow if stable. Will transition to warfarin for outpatient anticoagulation."
      },
      {
        "type": "Nursing Note",
        "author": "RN Jessica Torres",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-14T06:30:00",
        "title": "POD#6 Morning Assessment",
        "content": "Patient resting comfortably at start of shift. Vitals stable \u00e2\u20ac\u201d BP 132/78, HR 82, RR 16, SpO2 97%, T 37.1\u00c2\u00b0C. Surgical site clean, minimal drainage. Patient reported feeling 'okay but a little tired.' Morning labs drawn and sent. Heparin drip running at 18 units/kg/hr. Breakfast 50% eaten."
      },
      {
        "type": "Nursing Note",
        "author": "RN Jessica Torres",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-14T10:30:00",
        "title": "POD#6 Mid-Morning Update",
        "content": "Patient completed PT session but appeared more fatigued than yesterday. BP trending down to 118/72, HR up to 92. Patient noted to be slightly pale. Dark stool noted during toileting \u00e2\u20ac\u201d patient states this is new. Notified covering resident. Morning labs returned \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Hgb 8.2 (down from 10.1), platelets 89 (down from 132). Critical values reported to Dr. Zhao."
